It can mean that, but only in rare situations.  In a normal context
like this, a native speaker will always read "awful" as having a
negative meaning.

Jorgen Grahn says:
> Gee, that's an awful nice walkie-talkie you got there.

That's different.  As an adverb, "awful" (or, in more proper English,
"awfully") is just an intensifier (you can think of it as a synonym
for "very"), and the meaning comes from the word it modifies -- "nice"
in this case.

The adjective "awful" can *occasionally* have a positive or neutral
meaning, similar to "awesome" (well, similar to what "awesome" used to
mean before people started using it every other sentence a couple of
decades ago) and related to the literal sum of its parts ("awe" being
the real key), but this is rare and requires contextual clues.

In garden-variety situations, as an adjective, "awful" means "bad" or
"worse than bad".

